; What a Galaxian or Space Invaders frame costs on hardware, and whether the
; frame loop's storage stays flat while it runs.
;
;   erall                        <-- REQUIRED: see below
;   load "galaxian   (or "invaders -- do NOT run the game)
;   load "p10games
;   p10games
;
; Neither game had ever been timed on a board.  That is not an oversight
; peculiar to them: `sync` does not wait when a frame overruns, it presents
; late, so a game that misses its 25 fps budget degrades quietly instead of
; failing -- which is exactly how Turtle Trails and Checkpoint Run reached
; hardware at ~9 and ~4 fps against a designed 25 (P9 M0,
; docs/tilemap-scrolling-design.md section 3.3).  This script is the same
; measurement for the two older games, and it works on either of them
; unchanged because both now define `play.frame`, `init.game` and
; `setup.level` under those names.
;
; Manual refresh, so the `sync` ending a frame presents and returns rather
; than waiting for the frame boundary -- the number wanted is the work, not
; the cadence.  Run it in graphics mode (`setup.level` leaves the screen
; there): in text mode the present is skipped entirely and the figure is a
; Logo body with no present in it, which is the correction P9 had to make to
; its whole first series of numbers.
;
; `erall` is not optional.  MAX_PROCEDURES is a hard 128; Galaxian defines 74
; (Space Invaders 68) and this file adds 3, so a workspace still holding
; another program can run out of procedure slots and `load` stops with `out of
; space` -- the procedure table talking, not memory.
;
; The 300 frames are enough to cross one `reclaim` (the games recycle every
; 250), so the two `nodes` readings say whether a frame's storage comes back:
; before should be near after, and both far from zero.
;
; The report goes to `p10games.txt` as well as the screen, because numbers on
; the PicoCalc's display cannot be copied anywhere.  Runs append, so a session
; of them accumulates in one file; note beside each which game you loaded.

to time.frame
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  play.frame
  output (ticks - :t0)
end

; Which game is loaded, since the file appends and a run otherwise cannot be
; told from the one before it.  `conv.rows` is Galaxian's convoy and only
; Galaxian defines it; `alien.rows` is the Invaders formation.
to p10.game
  if name? "conv.rows [output [Galaxian]]
  if name? "alien.rows [output [Space Invaders]]
  output [unknown -- load a game first]
end

; Printed twice -- once to the file, once to the screen -- so it is one list of
; lines rather than two that can drift apart.  The measurements are the
; caller's locals, which Logo's dynamic scope makes visible here.
to p10.report
  pr (se [Game       :] p10.game)
  pr [Frame cost -- 300 frames, manual refresh]
  pr []
  pr (se [frame mean :] (:total / 300) [ms])
  pr (se [frame min  :] :lo [ms])
  pr (se [frame max  :] :hi [ms])
  pr [budget     : 40 ms at 25 fps]
  pr []
  pr (se [nodes at start :] :n0)
  pr (se [nodes at end   :] :n1)
  pr (se [after recycle  :] :n2)
  pr []
end

to p10games
  local "one local "total local "lo local "hi local "n0 local "n1 local "n2
  init.game
  make "score 0
  make "lives 3
  make "level 1
  setup.level
  setrefresh "manual
  recycle
  make "n0 nodes
  make "total 0
  make "lo 99999
  make "hi 0
  repeat 300 [
    make "one (time.frame)
    make "total :total + :one
    if :one < :lo [make "lo :one]
    if :one > :hi [make "hi :one]
  ]
  make "n1 nodes
  recycle
  ; Read the recovered figure *here*, not inside the report: printing the
  ; report builds a `sentence` per line, so a `nodes` evaluated down there
  ; reads the report's own garbage back as if the recycle had under-recovered
  ; (it showed ~25 cells low on the first hardware run, 2026-08-06).
  make "n2 nodes
  setrefresh "auto
  stopsound
  textscreen
  open "p10games.txt
  setwrite "p10games.txt
  p10.report
  setwrite []
  close "p10games.txt
  p10.report
  pr [Also written to p10games.txt]
end
